            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default ExploreHub;

payload.text:
DO NOT change UI design, colors, components, or navigation.

Convert the NutriForge app from a fixed mobile canvas layout into a fully responsive React JS web layout.

---

## STEP 1 — Remove Mobile Device Wrapper

Find the root container that limits width to mobile size (like 375px/390px) or shows phone frame.

Remove this wrapper completely.

The app must render directly inside the browser viewport.

---

## STEP 2 — Create Responsive Root Container

Wrap the entire app in:

<div className="app-container">

CSS:

.app-container {
  min-height: 100vh;
  width: 100%;
  background: #F5F6F8;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}

.inner-container {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 1200px;
  padding: 24px;
}

All screens must render inside `.inner-container`.

---

## STEP 3 — Make Layout Responsive

Replace any fixed widths like:

width: 360px
width: 375px

with:

width: 100%

Use Flexbox or Grid where cards are shown.

Example for cards:

.cards-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
  gap: 20px;
}

---

## STEP 4 — Make Bottom Navigation Responsive

On desktop/tablet:
Convert bottom navigation into a left sidebar.

On mobile:
Keep bottom navigation.

Use media query:

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  bottom-nav { display: none; }
  sidebar { display: block; width: 240px; }
}

---

## STEP 5 — Cards & Sections

Meal cards, recipe cards, grocery cards must expand fluidly to fill width.

No element should be constrained to mobile width.

---

## STEP 6 — Keep Same Styling

Do NOT change colors, typography, radius, buttons.
Only change layout behavior.

---

## RESULT

NutriForge becomes a proper responsive React JS web app:
- Desktop friendly
- Tablet friendly
- Mobile friendly
- No mobile frame

